This assumes Jenkins is now running at http://<VM-IP>:8080 and you’ve logged in. If not, finish the Java 21 fix first.

Step 0: Prerequisites on the VM

Jenkins needs the JDK, not just the JRE, to compile. The openjdk-21-jre package we installed has java but no javac.

bash
sudo apt install -y openjdk-21-jdk git
javac -version     # should print javac 21.x
git --version
Step 1: Create the Hello World app
bash
mkdir ~/hello-jenkins && cd ~/hello-jenkins

Create HelloWorld.java:

java
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}

Test it locally:

bash
javac HelloWorld.java
java HelloWorld

Now create the Jenkinsfile (no extension) in the same folder. This is the pipeline definition, and it lives in the repo with your code:

groovy
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout scm
            }
        }
        stage('Compile') {
            steps {
                sh 'javac HelloWorld.java'
            }
        }
        stage('Run') {
            steps {
                sh 'java HelloWorld | tee output.txt'
            }
        }
        stage('Verify') {
            steps {
                sh 'grep -q "Hello, World!" output.txt'
                echo 'Output verified: Hello, World! was printed.'
            }
        }
    }
}

What each stage does:

Checkout pulls the code from GitHub.
Compile runs javac, producing HelloWorld.class.
Run executes the program and saves its output to output.txt while still showing it in the console.
Verify fails the build if the expected text isn’t in the output. That’s the automated version of “check the console”.
Step 2: Push to GitHub
On github.com, click New repository, name it hello-jenkins, keep it Public (this avoids needing Jenkins credentials), and don’t add a README.
Back in the VM:
bash
cd ~/hello-jenkins
git init
git branch -M main
git config user.name "Your Name"
git config user.email "you@example.com"
git add .
git commit -m "Add HelloWorld and Jenkinsfile"
git remote add origin https://github.com/<your-username>/hello-jenkins.git
git push -u origin main

When prompted for a password, GitHub no longer accepts your account password. Create a Personal Access Token at GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens, with the repo scope, and paste it as the password.

Step 3: Create the Jenkins Pipeline job
Jenkins dashboard → New Item → name hello-world-pipeline → choose Pipeline → OK.
Scroll to Build Triggers and tick Poll SCM. In the Schedule box enter:
   H/2 * * * *

This makes Jenkins check GitHub every ~2 minutes and build only if there’s a new commit.
3. Scroll to Pipeline and set:

Definition: Pipeline script from SCM
SCM: Git
Repository URL: https://github.com/<your-username>/hello-jenkins.git
Branch Specifier: */main (Jenkins defaults to */master, which is the most common reason for a failed first build)
Script Path: Jenkinsfile
Save.
Step 4: Run it and verify
Click Build Now for the first run.
Open the build under Build History, then Console Output. You should see:
   + javac HelloWorld.java
   + java HelloWorld
   Hello, World!
   + grep -q Hello, World! output.txt
   Output verified: Hello, World! was printed.
   Finished: SUCCESS
The Stage View on the job page should show all four stages green.
Step 5: Prove Poll SCM works

Change the message in HelloWorld.java (for example "Hello, Jenkins!") and update the grep in the Jenkinsfile to match, then:

bash
git add . && git commit -m "Change greeting" && git push

Don’t click anything in Jenkins. Within about 2 minutes a new build starts on its own. Check Polling Log in the left menu of the job to see Jenkins detecting the change. That’s the trigger working.

Common problems
javac: command not found in the build: the JDK isn’t installed. Run sudo apt install openjdk-21-jdk, then sudo systemctl restart jenkins.
couldn't find any revision to build: the branch specifier is wrong. Use */main.
Poll SCM never triggers: the very first build must happen manually, and the schedule must be saved. Check the Polling Log for errors.
Git push asks for a password again and fails: use the token, not your GitHub password.

